WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.180
<v Instructor>Okay, so next, let's talk about VPC peering.</v>

2
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:07.470
We know the basics, VPC peering is used to connect two VPC,

3
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:11.400
privately, using the internal AWS network.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.210
And why do we do VPC peering?

5
00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.270
Well, because we make them behave,

6
00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.240
as if they were in the same Network.

7
00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.410
So the idea is that we have a VPC A and VPC B,

8
00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.580
and they have non overlapping CIDR,

9
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.980
and then we create a VPC peering connection.

10
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:26.760
And then we have to update some route tables,

11
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.490
but we allow the instances in VPC A,

12
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:33.890
to communicate privately, to VPC B,

13
00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:35.430
and vice versa.

14
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.590
Okay. For this, we must not have overlapping CIDR's,

15
00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.370
and we'll see this in a future lecture.

16
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:45.290
So the VPC peering connection is not transitive.

17
00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.300
This is something you should know as well,

18
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:48.460
which is the means that,

19
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:51.550
if you are establishing connection with another VPC,

20
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.820
for example, VPC C and you create a VPC peering connection,

21
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.216
between A and C because VPC peering is not a transitive,

22
00:00:59.216 --> 00:01:01.880
instances in VPC B

23
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.640
cannot talk to instances in VPC C.

24
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.370
That is just not allowed.

25
00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:11.130
For making sure that the VPC B can't talk to VPC C,

26
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.790
you need to create your own VPC peering connection,

27
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:16.730
between B and C for it to work.

28
00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.400
So you can do VPC peering in your own accounts,

29
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.920
but also with another alias accounts.

30
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.290
For this, remember, anytime you establish VPC peering,

31
00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:28.630
you must update route tables in each VPC subnet,

32
00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.710
to ensure the instances know what to talk to,

33
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.570
and where to go through the VPC peering connection.

34
00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.090
Okay, good to know.

35
00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.410
So VPC peering can work inter-region and cross-accounts.

36
00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.920
And then once it's established,

37
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.020
we can reference a security group have a peered VPC,

38
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.630
and that works cross accounts.

39
00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:49.690
So these rules are very helpful,

40
00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.730
for example here or saying, hey,

41
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.540
I want to allow Port 80 from this Security Group,

42
00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.470
of VPC in my other accounts,

43
00:01:57.470 --> 00:02:00.400
or VPC in my accounts, but a different VPC.

44
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.710
So using these rules,

45
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.890
we are able to reference Security Groups,

46
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.530
using other peer VPC and that makes security,

47
00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:08.410
very easy to manage.

48
00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.050
Next, the longest prefix match.

49
00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:13.840
So VPC uses something called a longest prefix match,

50
00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:17.060
to select the most specific route to route for.

51
00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.890
So let's have an example with VPC A,

52
00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:23.920
that is peered with both VPC B and C.

53
00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:25.230
And in this example,

54
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.340
we can look at if you can see in your screen,

55
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.110
a VPC B and VPC C are set up exactly the same way.

56
00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:34.070
And so they have the same CIDR,

57
00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:37.200
and they have the same subnet CIDR as well.

58
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.370
So for example,

59
00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:41.910
we definitely cannot peer VPC B and VPC C together,

60
00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:43.700
because they have overlapping CIDR.

61
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.620
But VPC A, doesn't have an overlapping CIDR with,

62
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.460
either VPC B or VPC C and therefore,

63
00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.070
we're able to create two VPC peering connections.

64
00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.900
Now, the problem is that we have a subnet A,

65
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.850
with a bunch of instances here,

66
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.070
and there's one instance in VPC B We'd like to access,

67
00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:04.950
which is 10.0.0 point 77 slash 32,

68
00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.720
which represents one IP.

69
00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.430
And then we want to be able to say,

70
00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.210
traffic that goes specifically to that instance,

71
00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:13.640
should go through VPCs B,

72
00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.160
but any other traffic should go through VPC C.

73
00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:20.350
And so the idea is that we're going to define route tables.

74
00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:21.980
And in the VPC A,

75
00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:24.920
we're going to say anything that is 172

76
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:26.440
is going to be local,

77
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.199
anything that is very specific,

78
00:03:29.199 --> 00:03:33.160
so 10 000 point 77 slash 32,

79
00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:35.000
is going to be the most specific route,

80
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.650
and is going to be sent to the VPC B,

81
00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.240
and then anything else 10 point 0.00 slash 16

82
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.560
is going to go to VPC C.

83
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.850
The idea is that this route here,

84
00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:49.610
slash 32 is the most specific it is the longest prefix,

85
00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.090
and therefore it's going to be having priority,

86
00:03:52.090 --> 00:03:54.860
in case there's a match or the second rule.

87
00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.510
And then the other VPC,

88
00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.360
have just a basic VPC peering connection.

89
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.610
So once you understand this,

90
00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.480
that means that anything that's going to be the most

91
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:03.313
specific route,

92
00:04:03.313 --> 00:04:06.920
is going to have precedence over more larger routes.

93
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.700
This is very intuitive, but it's nice to point out.

94
00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:13.330
Okay, now, what are some valid configurations,

95
00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:14.780
for VPC peering?

96
00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.030
The first one is overlapping CIDR for IPV4.

97
00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:21.360
So in case we have two VPC and they share one CIDR,

98
00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.670
they cannot connect.

99
00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.470
That is true if even one CIDR out of the many CIDRs

100
00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.980
defined in the VPC overlap.

101
00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.980
So in this example, 10.3.0.0 and 10.2.0.0 don't overlap,

102
00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:38.040
but because there is another CIDR in EGP C that overlap,

103
00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.560
then we cannot introduce VPC peering.

104
00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.030
And this is also true for IPV6.

105
00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.080
So as long as there's one IPV4,

106
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.080
overlap between the VPC A and B,

107
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:48.680
you cannot peer them even if they have

108
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:51.090
different IPV6 CIDR's.

109
00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.230
The second thing is that there is no transitive VPC peering.

110
00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.620
So we've seen this configuration it does not work.

111
00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.430
So if you have a VPC B peered with A and A peered with C,

112
00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:02.450
Then B and C cannot connect.

113
00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.490
They have to be peered to one another.

114
00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:07.580
But also there's something called no Edge to Edge routing,

115
00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:09.950
and that may be a little bit new to you.

116
00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:11.170
So what does it mean?

117
00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:13.250
We have this example where VPC B,

118
00:05:14.201 --> 00:05:15.650
and VPC A are peered together.

119
00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.880
And VPC A is linked to your corporate network,

120
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.050
using either direct connect or a VPN connection,

121
00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.440
whatever you want, right?

122
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.200
In this example, VPC B can connect to VPC A,

123
00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.620
but it cannot connect to the corporate network,

124
00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.180
through VPC A.

125
00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:34.630
The reason is there's no edge to edge routing.

126
00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:35.900
And this is also true.

127
00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.630
If you're using VPC B peered with VPC A.

128
00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:40.913
And there's an internet gateway in VPC A.

129
00:05:41.859 --> 00:05:42.692
It doesn't work.

130
00:05:42.692 --> 00:05:45.260
VPC B will not be able to access the Internet,

131
00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:47.740
through the internet gateway or VPC A.

132
00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:51.330
So no extra edge routing applies to site to site VPN,

133
00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:55.030
Direct Connect Internet gateways, NAT gateways,

134
00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:58.850
and gateway VPC endpoints for S3 and DynamoD8.

135
00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.340
So all these things, you should remember.

136
00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:01.720
So let's do a deep dive.

137
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:03.950
Okay, so let's go through this example,

138
00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:07.010
where someone comes up in your company and say yes,

139
00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:09.250
I would like to have a central VPC,

140
00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.310
and a central NAT gateway,

141
00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:12.960
and that NAT gateway is going to be connected to,

142
00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.440
an Internet gateway to connect to the Internet.

143
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.320
And then I want to have multiple VPC,

144
00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.350
connect to the Internet gateway through my central VPC.

145
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.130
And that may seem like a good idea.

146
00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:26.400
So let's go ahead we have a VPC and a private subnet.

147
00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.910
And we're going to do a VPC peering connection.

148
00:06:28.910 --> 00:06:30.950
And will it work?

149
00:06:30.950 --> 00:06:33.610
Well, no, this is an invalid configuration.

150
00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.610
It will not allow your private subnets,

151
00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.970
to talk to the Internet's gateway,

152
00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:40.710
because there is no Edge to Edge routing,

153
00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.760
for NAT devices through VPC peering.

154
00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.350
And that is something you have to remember

155
00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:46.360
going to the exam.

156
00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:49.210
I will give you a solution to that problem later on.

157
00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:52.140
But this is an invalid configuration.

158
00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.230
And so no matter how many VPCs you're trying to connect to

159
00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:56.430
the central VPC,

160
00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.680
if you have defined VPC peering,

161
00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.880
then there will be no Edge to Edge routing,

162
00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.740
and so you cannot leverage a NAT gateway,

163
00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:05.260
to talk to the Internet gateway,

164
00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.940
to have a central traffic going through the central VPC.

165
00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.350
We will see how to solve this problem in the next lecture.

166
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:13.280
So that's it for VPC peering.

167
00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:14.113
I hope you liked it,

168
00:07:14.113 --> 00:07:15.150
Hope you learned a thing or two,

169
00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.100
and I will see you in the next lecture.

